  "answer": "**Trace of `console.print(\"Hello [bold]world[/bold]\")`**\n\n1. **Entry and buffering.** `Console.print` (`rich/console.py:1646`) enters `with self:` at line 1698, which calls `__enter__` (`console.py:851`) and `_enter_buffer` (`console.py:807`). That increments `_buffer_index`, so nothing is written yet.\n\n2. **Markup to Text.** `_collect_renderables` (`console.py:1494`) passes each string to `render_str` (`console.py:1554`). When markup is on, `render_str` calls `markup.render` (`console.py:1436`, defined at `rich/markup.py:106`). `_parse` (`markup.py:73`, regex `RE_TAGS` at `markup.py:12`) yields `Hello ` as plain text and `[bold]`/`[/bold]` as tags. Opening tags go on a stack (`markup.py:223`), and closing tags become a `Span(start, end, \"bold\")` (`markup.py:219`). The spans are attached to the Text at `markup.py:232`.\n\n3. **Render to Segments.** Optional render hooks run (`console.py:1708`), then `self.render` (`console.py:1288`) calls `Text.__rich_console__` (`rich/text.py:677`). That wraps the text and calls `Text.render` (`text.py:707`), which splits the text at span boundaries, resolves each span's style, and yields `Segment(text, style)` objects.\n\n4. **Buffer.** Segments are split and cropped to width and appended to `self._buffer` (`console.py:1745\u20131748`).\n\n5. **Flush.** Leaving `with self:` calls `__exit__` (`console.py:856`) \u2192 `_exit_buffer` (`console.py:811`) \u2192 `_check_buffer` (`console.py:2038`) \u2192 `_write_buffer` (`console.py:2053`). Once `_buffer_index == 0`, it calls `_render_buffer` (`console.py:2116`). For each styled segment that method calls `Style.render` (`rich/style.py:694`), which wraps the text as `\\x1b[1mworld\\x1b[0m`. Unstyled text passes through unchanged (`console.py:2135\u20132145`).\n\n6. **Write.** `self.file.write(text)` (`console.py:2118`), then `self.file.flush()` (`console.py:2123`), then the buffer is cleared (`console.py:2124`).\n\n**Windows branch:** on legacy Windows consoles with a standard stream, `legacy_windows_render` is used instead (`console.py:2077\u20132085`), which calls the Win32 API rather than writing ANSI codes.\n\nI read these paths directly and did not run the code, so the behaviour is traced from source only.",
